const faq = [
  { q: 'Are ravens afraid of eagles?', a: 'Ravens show pronounced alarm responses to large raptors including eagles, but they also mob them aggressively. Ravens have been documented driving off Golden Eagles and even riding on the backs of flying eagles in what may be play or displacement behaviour. The relationship is one of mutual wariness rather than simple predator–prey.' },
  { q: 'Do ravens have any defence against predators?', a: 'Yes. Ravens\' primary defences are vigilance, group mobbing, and their own intelligence. They are alert birds that give alarm calls and recruit other ravens to mob potential threats. Nesting on inaccessible cliff ledges is a structural defence. Adults\' large size, strong bill, and aggressive behaviour deter most would-be predators.' },
  { q: 'Do humans predate ravens?', a: 'Historically, ravens were killed in large numbers across Europe and North America — shot, poisoned, and trapped — because of perceived threats to livestock and game. Legal protection has reversed this in most countries (UK Wildlife and Countryside Act 1981, US MBTA 1918), and illegal persecution still occurs but is now the main human-related mortality factor rather than systematic killing.' },
];
