    <h1>What Eats Ravens? Raven Predators Explained</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Adult ravens have few natural predators — their large size, intelligence, and mobbing behaviour protect them. The main threats are large raptors (Golden Eagles, Gyrfalcons, Great Horned Owls) and mammalian predators at the nest. Eggs and nestlings are more vulnerable than adults, and young ravens in their first year face the highest mortality of their lives.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Raptors — the Main Avian Predators</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Large birds of prey are the most significant avian predators of ravens. The <em>Birds of the World</em> account for Common Raven documents the following:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Golden Eagle (<em>Aquila chrysaetos</em>):</strong> documented killing adult Common Ravens, particularly in open country where ravens cannot easily escape or recruit help. Ravens mob Golden Eagles vigorously, but a large eagle can overpower an adult raven.</li>
      <li><strong>Gyrfalcon (<em>Falco rusticolus</em>):</strong> in Arctic and sub-Arctic environments, the Gyrfalcon is a confirmed raven predator. Raven remains have been found in Gyrfalcon nests.</li>
      <li><strong>Great Horned Owl (<em>Bubo virginianus</em>) and Eagle Owl (<em>Bubo bubo</em>):</strong> large owls can take roosting ravens at night when the birds cannot respond to threats. Great Horned Owls in North America are cited as a raven predator in several studies.</li>
      <li><strong>Red-tailed Hawk (<em>Buteo jamaicensis</em>) and other Buteos:</strong> occasionally attack juvenile or weakened ravens, but adult Common Ravens are typically too large and aggressive for most buteos.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Nest Predators</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The nest — typically on a cliff ledge or in a large tree — is the most vulnerable point in the raven's life cycle. Known nest predators include:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup><sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Martens and Wolverines:</strong> in boreal forests, pine marten (<em>Martes martes</em>) and wolverine (<em>Gulo gulo</em>) can reach tree nests and consume eggs or chicks. Cliff nests are more secure.</li>
      <li><strong>Red Fox and other canids:</strong> can reach accessible ground-level or low-tree nests, though cliff-nesting ravens are largely safe from foxes.</li>
      <li><strong>American Black Bear and Brown Bear:</strong> bears are powerful nest predators capable of reaching cliff-ledge nests in some situations and tree nests routinely.</li>
      <li><strong>Other corvids:</strong> Ravens vigorously defend their nests from other corvids. Crows and other ravens may raid unguarded nests.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Why Adults Are Rarely Taken</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Adult raven annual survival rates in studied populations range from 72–87%, meaning most adults survive each year. Several factors explain their relative safety:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Size:</strong> at 0.7–1.6 kg with a heavy bill, an adult Common Raven is a formidable bird. Few predators can subdue a healthy adult.</li>
      <li><strong>Intelligence and vigilance:</strong> ravens are observant and respond rapidly to threats. They maintain sentinels when foraging in groups.</li>
      <li><strong>Mobbing:</strong> ravens will collectively mob raptors and even drive them from territories — behaviour documented in many field studies.</li>
      <li><strong>Habitat selection:</strong> cliff nesting, roost site selection, and avoidance of open ground reduce exposure to ambush predators.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>First-Year Mortality</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The most dangerous period of a raven's life is the first year. Estimates of first-year survival in Common Ravens range from about 20–50% depending on population and habitat. This high mortality is not primarily from predation but from starvation, accidents, and disease — young ravens lack the food-finding skills of adults and must compete with them in non-breeding flocks.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Human-Caused Mortality</h2>
    <p>Beyond traditional persecution (now illegal in most countries), the main human-related mortality factors for ravens today are: illegal shooting and poisoning (still documented in UK upland game estates), vehicle collisions (ravens often feed on road-killed animals), power-line electrocution, and secondary poisoning from lead shot and rodenticide-contaminated prey. These are not predation in the ecological sense, but they are significant mortality sources in some populations.</p>
  </section>

